# retrieval.co — RETRIEVAL CONTROLS (Colang 1.0)
#
# CAVEAT — read before editing: NeMo Guardrails' native retrieval-rail
# mechanism (relevant_chunks, retrieve_relevant_chunks action) assumes
# guardrails itself performs retrieval. In this stack, Open WebUI performs
# RAG retrieval (via Chroma/Qdrant + the embedder service) and hands
# guardrails an already context-stuffed prompt — guardrails never sees
# discrete "chunks" as a first-class object, only the assembled chat
# message. The flows below are therefore *context-aware approximations*:
# they pattern-match on Open WebUI's injected context block rather than
# implementing true chunk-grounded retrieval rails. Confirm the exact
# context-block delimiter against your Open WebUI version's RAG_TEMPLATE at
# deployment time (checked here as "<context>", matching the documented
# default as of authoring time).

# ── Scenario C target: "information not found" short-circuit ────────────────
# Runs as an INPUT rail (see config.yml) specifically so it can stop before
# the vLLM generation call when there is effectively nothing to answer from
# — this is the most reliable version of this check precisely because it
# doesn't depend on inspecting the LLM's own (possibly hallucinated) output.
define flow low confidence refusal
  $has_context_block = "<context>" in $user_message
  $context_start = $user_message.find("<context>")
  $context_end = $user_message.find("</context>")

  # Only evaluate emptiness when Open WebUI actually attached a context
  # block (i.e. RAG was active for this turn / a knowledge base is
  # attached). Plain chat turns with no KB attached are not subject to this
  # check — see PLAIN vs RAG task distinction in locustfile.py for the same
  # convention used by this repo's own benchmarking.
  if $has_context_block
    $context_length = $context_end - $context_start

    if $context_length < 40
      bot inform answer not found in knowledge base
      stop

# ── Answer only from retrieved content ───────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberately NOT a separate Colang flow here: a Colang flow that only sets
# a local variable can't rewrite the outbound prompt or reliably ground the
# model's answer, and shipping one that looks like it does would be
# misleading dead code. "Answer only from retrieved content" is instead
# implemented as the combination of two flows that ARE functional:
#   1. `low confidence refusal` above — refuses before generation when
#      there's no context to ground an answer in at all.
#   2. `self check output` in output.co — its self_check_output prompt
#      (see config.yml prompts:) explicitly asks the LLM to flag "confident
#      factual claims that read as fabricated ... rather than being
#      grounded in information the user actually provided."
# If stricter grounding is needed later, the correct extension point is a
# custom action (see actions.py's pattern) that checks bot_message content
# against the extracted <context> block, analogous to filter_sensitive_info.
